Process: Process is an instance of a program execution. When you enter an application it's a process. The OS assigns
it's own stack and heap memory area.

Thread: It is a lightweight process. It is a unit of execution within a given program. A single process may contain
multiple threads. Each thread in the process shares the memory and resources.

Concurrency: Concurrency means dealing with multiple tasks at once, but not necessarily executing them simultaneously.
Instead, tasks make progress by sharing time on the same processing resource. Concurrency is an approach that is used
for decreasing the response time of the system by using the single processing unit. It creates the illusion of parallelism.

Parallelism: Parallelism refers to actually executing multiple tasks at the same time on multiple processing units.
Here  tasks are divided into smaller sub-tasks that are processed seemingly simultaneously or parallel. It is used to
increase the throughput and computational speed of the system by using multiple processors.

Thread Lifecycle:
    New state: Every thread is in this state until we call start() on it.
    Active State: Every thread is in this state after we call start() on it. It has 2 sub states running and runnable.
    Blocked state: Every thread is in this state when it's waiting for some thread to finish.
    Terminated state: Every thread is in this state after it's done doing it's required task.

Runnable vs Thread:
    -> If we extend Thread then we cannot extend any other class, usually a big disadvantage.
    -> However, a class may implement more than one interface, so while using Implements Runnable approach there is no
       restrictions to extension of class now or in the future.

join method in java:
    Main thread as the parent method
        When we start a program usually, the execution begins with main() method. This method runs on the main thread.
        This can be understood as the parent thread since it spawns the other threads.
    Independent execution of threads
        When you create and start other threads, they run concurrently with the main threads unless instructed otherwise.
        So under normal circumstances, all threads run independent of each other. More explicitly, no thread waits for other
        thread
    What is join method:
        Imagine threads to be lines of execution. So, when we call .join() method on a certain thread, it means the parent thread,
        the main thread in this case (could be any thread which created the thread on which .join() is begin called) is
        saying "Hey thread, once you are done executing your task, join my flow of execution". It's like the parent thread waits
        for the completion of the child thread and then continues with its execution.

Daemon and User Threads:
    -> On the basis of surface of execution, threads can of 2 types: Daemon and User Threads
    -> When a java program starts the main thread (main method thread) starts running immediately. We can start child
       threads from the main thread. The main thread is the last thread to finish execution in normal circumstances, because
       it has to perform various shutdown operations,
    -> Daemon threads are intended to be helper threads which can run in background and are of low priority.
    -> Daemon threads are terminated by JVM when all other user threads are terminated or done with their execution.
    -> So, under normal circumstances, user thread are allowed to terminate once they are done with their execution, however,
       the daemon threads are shutdown by JVM once all the other threads are done executing.

Note: By default threads are not daemon threads. We need to set it using thread_name.setDaemon(true)

Thread Priority:
-> Each thread has certain priority and under normal circumstances the thread with the higher priority gets to ubn on the CPU.
-> Priority values ranges from 1 to 10. 1 is represented as MIN_PRIORITY and 10 as MAX_PRIORITY.
-> threads with same priority are executed in FIFO manner. Thread scheduler stores the threads in a queue.
Note: We set priority using the method thread_name.setPriority();

Thread Synchronization:
-> To prevent race condition.
Using synchronized keyword.
Note: When we use synchronized keyword for a method then class level lock is acquired, thus if there is another method
which is synchronized, then it has to wait until the first method has released the class level lock. Thus, it is advised to
use synchronize keyword at block level instead of method level.

Wait and Notify:
Note: Difference between wait and sleep: wait is used to inter thread communication and synchronization, but sleep is used to
pause a thread for a specified duration.
-> wait(): The wait() method causes the current thread to pause its execution and release the lock (monitor) it holds on the object.
-> notify(): The notify() method wakes up a single thread that is currently waiting on the object's monitor.
   If multiple threads are waiting, only one arbitrarily chosen thread will be awakened.

Note: The thread that called notify() continues to hold the object's monitor lock. It does not release this lock until
it exits the synchronized block or method in which notify was called, or until it explicitly calls wait() itself.

Producer Consumer: The producer consumer problem is a synchronization scenario where one or more producer threads generate data
and put it into a shared buffer, while one or more consumer threads retrieve and process the data from the buffer concurrently.

Executor Service: Helps us in creating a bunch of threads, a pool of threads hence the name thread pool and the threads are not
killed once they are done executing the task, rather they are reused to execute another task.

Types:
    -> SingleThreadExecutor
    -> FixedThreadPoolExecutor
    -> CachedThreadPool
    -> ScheduledExecutor

Note: The executor service has to be closed once we are done using it.

SingleThreadExecutor: In the case of a SingleThreadExecutor, the size of the thread pool is just one. The order of task
execution is always followed.

FixedThreadPoolExecutor: In the case of FixedThreadPool, there is a fixed number of threads that are pre created and the executor
also has a task queue where all the tasks will be placed. The threads in the fixed thread pool will pick the tasks from the
task queue and execute them, and when the thread is done with the execution of a task, it goes ahead and picks another task
and this keeps on happening until all the tasks are completed.

CachedThreadPoolExecutor: In the case of CachedThreadPoolExecutor, we don't have a fixed number of threads, the task queue
does not hold the number of tasks which we submit. The queue used in this case is a synchronous queue. The synchronous queue
has space only for a single task, so every time you submit a new task the CachedThreadPoolExecutor holds the task in the
synchronous queue and it searches for the thread which has been already created and they are not working actively on any task.
If no such thread is available the executor will create a new thread and add it to the thread pool and this newly created thread
starts to execute the task which has been submitted. If a task is not assigned to a thread within 60 seconds,
then the thread will be killed. So CachedThreadPoolExecutor is auto scaling in nature.

ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor: This thread pool is used for kind of tasks which we want to schedule in some manner and
they way it works is all the tasks are submitted in a task queue, but this queue is a delay queue, in the delay queue,
tasks are not kept in sequential manner, but it is ordered based on the execution time.


